1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: And what's left are the prime numbers.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: Though our earliest record of the sieve was written centuries later, and he may have named it rather than invented it.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: Later writers used smaller figures for the Earth. Ptolemy's Geography used just a hundred and eighty thousand stadia.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: And some seventeen centuries after Eratosthenes, Columbus read his work, but chose to believe in an Earth about a quarter smaller.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: And a smaller Earth made the voyage west to Asia look shorter.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: So let's put the whole idea in one picture.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: No shadow at Syene. A shadow of one fiftieth of a circle at Alexandria.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: Parallel rays carry that angle to the centre of the Earth.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: Fifty times five thousand stadia: two hundred and fifty thousand stadia round.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: How close was he? That depends on a unit we still can't pin down.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: But the idea was right, and it measured the whole Earth from two cities in Egypt.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: With a stick, a bowl and a good argument.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: Not bad for a man his critics called Beta.
